---
import MotionFigure from "./MotionFigure.astro";
import Figure from "./Figure.astro";
import { localPath } from "../lib/paths";
import { applications } from "../data/excavator";
---
<section id="research" class="research-section frame">
  <div class="section-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">01 / Our research</p>
    <div class="intro-grid">
      <h2>From an operator’s<br />experience to a<br /><em>robot’s skill.</em></h2>
      <div class="intro-copy"><p>Excavation is more than moving a bucket. The robot must understand the worksite, choose a useful motion, and reach a target as the terrain changes.</p><p>Our approach starts with teleoperation. An operator demonstrates the task; we turn the recording into keyframes and use them to teach the robot how to move.</p></div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="demonstrations" class="demonstration-sequence">
    <article><div class="step-label"><span>01</span> Demonstrate <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div><MotionFigure file="operator-control" title="The operator leads.">Teleoperation connects the operator’s input to the robot.</MotionFigure></article>
    <article><div class="step-label"><span>02</span> Capture <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div><MotionFigure file="teleoperation-testbed" title="The system observes.">Record robot motion alongside visual observations of the task.</MotionFigure></article>
    <article><div class="step-label"><span>03</span> Execute <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></div><MotionFigure file="autonomous-excavation" title="The robot performs the dig.">After learning, the laboratory robot executes an excavation motion.</MotionFigure></article>
  </div>
  <p class="sequence-note">Teleoperation and autonomous execution on the research testbed.</p>
</section>

<section id="method" class="keyframes-section">
  <div class="frame keyframe-grid">
    <div class="keyframe-copy"><p class="eyebrow">Learning what matters</p><h2>A demonstration is dense.<br /><em>The lesson needn’t be.</em></h2><p>Human demonstrations contain pauses and extra movements. Keyframe selection keeps the essential poses from task-completing trajectories. A high-level policy proposes subgoals; a low-level policy learns the actions between them.</p><div class="keyframe-stat"><strong>&gt;80<span>%</span></strong><div>of redundant frames removed<a href="https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10533657#page=32">Reported in the paper ↗</a></div></div></div>
    <div class="keyframe-media"><p class="figure-label">A demonstrated trajectory → selected keyframes</p><a class="figure-link" data-zoom href={localPath("images/keyframe-extraction.png")} aria-label="Enlarge keyframe extraction figure"><img src={localPath("images/keyframe-extraction.png")} width="1397" height="611" loading="lazy" alt="Paper figure showing a digging trajectory with selected keyframes marked as red dots" /><span class="enlarge-hint">↗ Enlarge</span></a><p class="visual-caption">The red points are the keyframes. <a href="https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10533657#page=20">Fig. 3 ↗</a></p><details class="technical-detail"><summary>Explore the learning framework <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><Figure file="method-framework.png" alt="Framework connecting keyframe identification, visual representation, goal generation and hierarchical policy learning" width={1430} height={629} paperFigure={1} page={12}>Selected subgoals guide the learned motion policy.</Figure></details></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="results" class="results-section">
  <div class="frame">
    <div class="intro-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">02 / From learning to action</p><h2>Give the robot<br /><em>a goal to work toward.</em></h2></div><div class="intro-copy"><p>Excavation changes the scene after every pass. The framework uses either a target contour or a goal generated in a compact visual representation to guide the next trajectory.</p><p class="scope-label">Validated in simulation and on laboratory robotic arms.</p></div></div>
    <div class="results-grid">
      <div class="execution-demo" data-tabs>
        <div class="line-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Excavation goal formulation"><button type="button" role="tab" id="goal-contour-tab" aria-controls="goal-contour" aria-selected="true">Target contour</button><button type="button" role="tab" id="goal-vision-tab" aria-controls="goal-vision" aria-selected="false" tabindex="-1">Visual goal</button></div>
        <div id="goal-contour" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="goal-contour-tab" tabindex="0"><MotionFigure file="contour-map-goal" title="A specified terrain target.">A desired site contour supplies the excavation goal.</MotionFigure></div>
        <div id="goal-vision" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="goal-vision-tab" tabindex="0" hidden><MotionFigure file="visual-latent-goal" title="A goal generated from the scene." autoplay={false}>The visual model generates a target in its learned latent space.</MotionFigure></div>
      </div>
      <aside class="result-evidence"><p class="eyebrow">Connecting vision and action</p><div class="success-stat"><span>40%</span><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span><strong>&gt;60%</strong></div><h3>Excavation success in the representation study.</h3><p>Binding visual features to robot actions improved success compared with a pure autoencoder.</p><a href="https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10533657#page=33">Read the experiment ↗</a><p class="scope-note">These are study results, not full-size machine or jobsite performance figures.</p></aside>
    </div>
    <details class="technical-detail result-detail"><summary>See the comparison and test setup <span aria-hidden="true">+</span></summary><div class="expanded-results"><Figure file="policy-results.png" alt="Paper comparison of robot learning methods and their success rate across training timesteps" width={1371} height={607} paperFigure={8} page={28}>Learning performance compared with the paper’s baseline methods.</Figure><Figure file="paper-testbed.png" alt="The paper's laboratory excavation setup with robotic arm, sandbox, and RGB-D cameras" width={701} height={743} paperFigure={6} page={24}>The research platform used for laboratory validation.</Figure></div></details>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="applications" class="applications-section frame">
  <div class="section-intro"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Where this could lead</p><div class="intro-grid"><h2>One learned skill.<br /><em>A wider field of work.</em></h2><div class="intro-copy"><p>The contribution is a way to learn construction motions from people. Adapting that approach opens questions beyond a single dig.</p><p class="scope-label">Research directions · Each needs its own hardware, sensing, and validation.</p></div></div></div>
  <div class="application-explorer" data-tabs>
    <div class="application-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Potential applications">
      {applications.map((app, i) => <button type="button" id={`application-${app.id}-tab`} role="tab" aria-controls={`application-${app.id}`} aria-selected={i === 0 ? "true" : "false"} tabindex={i === 0 ? 0 : -1}><span>{app.number}</span>{app.label}<span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></button>)}
    </div>
    {applications.map((app, i) => <div id={`application-${app.id}`} class="application-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby={`application-${app.id}-tab`} tabindex="0" hidden={i !== 0}>
      <figure class={`application-image application-image-${app.id}`}><img src={localPath(`images/earth-beyond/${app.image}`)} alt={app.alt} width="1440" height="1080" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>{app.caption}</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="application-copy"><p class="eyebrow">{app.number} / Potential extension</p><h3>{app.title}</h3><p>{app.text}</p><div class="next-test"><span>What it takes</span><p>{app.next}</p></div><a href={app.source}>{app.sourceLabel} ↗</a></div>
    </div>)}
  </div>
  <div class="field-note"><span class="eyebrow">From the testbed to the field</span><p>Full-scale deployment still needs machine calibration, richer demonstrations, integrated sensing, and supervised trials in changing site conditions.</p></div>
</section>
